5 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348035 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348035 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f579af6d-d006-5c83-962e-5ae07618e854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ac549bb-41f9-5150-895e-d407a77f8e37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348065 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bbd6a2b-6a4a-5f26-8d86-72b22a64837d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348342 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b478a4e0-e373-5d4c-8f17-c97eaae0c7f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348343 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1071 "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9f0ee2e-ecf1-5ebf-8f05-484d1b3df712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348344 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1071 "$2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)≈$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6b79f0a-30fd-5a34-a89c-78169fdf9be8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348348 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.955\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =8+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =24 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0489168e-93f4-5458-8a02-7bdcd1fa6253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348520 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08de7cad-4f07-5f29-802a-a2853256d6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348703 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348703 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1