] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186350374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "674d76e0-0dad-52e1-9b92-041ef1633c77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350902 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "71561c41-3170-56ee-aacf-b2d7928f8574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7d8a5d1-5161-58c0-bd9b-fe0499d92ff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351254 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{Re}(k)$ given that\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-4+8\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$\\overset{―}{kv}=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -4+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0aa00a7d-3087-5697-a956-d960345470c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351388 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\overset{―}{kv}=\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{v},$ we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{kv} & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{v} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -4+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}-4+8\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -4+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{-4+8\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -4+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -4+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-4-8\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k} \\\\ 4\\overset{―}{k}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-8\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k}=20 \\\\ 4\\overset{―}{k}=-4+8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{k}=-1+2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain the true statement\n\n$$\n(-4-8\\text{i})(-1+2\\text{i})=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\overset{―}{-1+2\\text{i}}=-1-2\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, $\\text{Re}(k)=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351566 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0065af71-29a6-5ebd-9bb2-e8f6fd53309c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351571 #edb/ref 1080 "A rotation of $\\frac{5\\pi }